{"id": "E4", "kind": "learn", "question": "Is the function exactly linear (y = a*x + b) across the full [-128,128] range, or does it bend/saturate near the extremes like an MLP often does?", "expect": "If linear, y \u2248 4.76x + 3.9 everywhere. Deviations at |x|>100 (e.g., flattening or steeper rise) would reveal the MLP's nonlinearity/saturation region and let me add a correction term. Values near \u00b1128 test whether the network's output clamps or bends at the domain edge; interior points re-check the slope/intercept. This is the most informative single probe because the current model is a pure line and would differ most from an MLP at the extremes.", "probe": {"x": [-128, -120, -110, -100, -90, -70, -50, -25, 0, 25, 50, 70, 90, 100, 110, 120, 128, 127, -127, 64]}, "observation": {"y": [-595.6816, -558.1884, -511.322, -464.4556, -417.5892, -323.8564, -230.1235, -112.9575, 3.9155, 122.0635, 239.9148, 334.1958, 428.4769, 475.6174, 522.7579, 569.8984, 607.6108, 602.8968, -590.9949, 305.9115]}, "cost": 20, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [-609.28, -571.1999999999999, -523.6, -476.0, -428.4, -333.2, -238.0, -119.0, 0.0, 119.0, 238.0, 333.2, 428.4, 476.0, 523.6, 571.1999999999999, 609.28, 604.52, -604.52, 304.64]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
